**assistant**:
> Yes: if Whoosh is disposable, **don’t rebuild it on every `remote-update` unless you need to**. Treat it as a local cache derived from canonical data/logs.
> 
> My recommended policy:
> 
> 1. **Never sync or Git-track `database/whoosh/`.**
>    Add:
> 
>    ```gitignore
>    database/whoosh/
>    ```
> 
>    and remove it from Git:
> 
>    ```bash
>    git -C "$LOCAL_PATH" rm -r --cached database/whoosh
>    git -C "$LOCAL_PATH" commit -m "Stop tracking generated Whoosh index"
>    ```
> 
> 2. **Do not include the whole `database` directory in `FILE_PATHS`.**
>    Instead sync only canonical files. If `database/whoosh` is inside `database`, then this is risky:
> 
>    ```bash
>    FILE_PATHS=( "database" )
>    ```
> 
>    Better:
> 
>    ```bash
>    FILE_PATHS=(
>        "knowledge/entries.txt"
>        "memory/bayesian.txt"
>        # specific canonical database files only
>    )
>    ```
> 
> 3. **Update Whoosh incrementally daily.**
>    Since you already process logs into Whoosh, keep doing that. The daily deploy should sync logs/canonical memory, then run something like:
> 
>    ```bash
>    venv/bin/python scripts/update_whoosh.py
>    ```
> 
>    where `update_whoosh.py` only indexes new/unprocessed records.
> 
> 4. **Full rebuild only occasionally.**
>    I’d rebuild Whoosh when:
>    - the index schema changes,
>    - the indexing code changes materially,
>    - the index is corrupted,
>    - you suspect drift,
>    - or on a scheduled weekly/monthly maintenance run.
> 
>    Daily full rebuild is probably unnecessary unless it is fast enough and the site can tolerate it.
> 
> 5. **Make rebuild atomic.**
>    Rebuild into a temporary directory, then swap:
> 
>    ```text
>    database/whoosh.tmp/
>    database/whoosh/
>    ```
> 
>    so the app never sees a half-built index.
> 
> 6. **Use a lock.**
>    Whoosh does not love multiple writers. Your updater/rebuilder should take a lock so the web app and deployment script do not write simultaneously.
> 
> A good deployment pattern would be:
> 
> ```bash
> # sync bidirectional logs/canonical memory
> # pull code
> # install deps
> venv/bin/python scripts/update_whoosh.py --incremental
> ```
> 
> Then perhaps separately, from cron:
> 
> ```bash
> venv/bin/python scripts/rebuild_whoosh.py --atomic
> ```
> 
> weekly or monthly.
> 
> My opinion: Whoosh should be treated exactly like `__pycache__`, thumbnails, or compiled assets: important for performance, but disposable and locally regenerated.
