**assistant**:
> Yes: replacing the server venv is the right move, but do it **atomically-ish and explicitly**, not by trying to mutate a 3.10 venv into 3.11+. Python venvs are tied to the interpreter they were created with.
> 
> Recommended server-side process:
> 
> ```bash
> ssh existencia
> cd /home/jrising/projects/arachne.existencia.org
> 
> # Confirm available Python versions.
> python3 --version
> python3.11 --version  # or python3.12, etc.
> ```
> 
> If `python3.11` exists:
> 
> ```bash
> # Stop/restart-sensitive apps if needed, or rely on Passenger restart later.
> 
> # Preserve old venv temporarily.
> mv venv venv.py310.$(date -u +%Y%m%dT%H%M%SZ)
> 
> # Create fresh venv with the desired interpreter.
> python3.11 -m venv venv
> 
> # Install dependencies.
> venv/bin/python -m pip install --upgrade pip setuptools wheel
> venv/bin/python -m pip install -r requirements.txt
> 
> # Restart Passenger.
> touch tmp/restart.txt
> ```
> 
> Then test:
> 
> ```bash
> venv/bin/python --version
> venv/bin/python -m pip check
> curl --fail --silent --show-error https://arachne.existencia.org/checkupdate
> ```
> 
> I’d also update your deployment script to enforce the required Python version instead of silently using whatever `python3` means:
> 
> ```bash
> REMOTE_PYTHON="python3.11"
> ```
> 
> Pass it into the remote deployment block, then replace:
> 
> ```bash
> python3 -m venv venv
> ```
> 
> with:
> 
> ```bash
> if ! command -v "$remote_python" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
>     echo "Required Python not found on remote: $remote_python" >&2
>     exit 1
> fi
> 
> if [[ ! -x "venv/bin/python" ]] ||
>    [[ "$(venv/bin/python -c 'import sys; print(f"{sys.version_info.major}.{sys.version_info.minor}")')" != "3.11" ]]; then
>     echo "Recreating remote virtual environment with $remote_python"
>     rm -rf venv
>     "$remote_python" -m venv venv
> fi
> ```
> 
> My opinion: pin the deployment to **3.11 or 3.12**, not 3.14, unless you have a specific reason. Python 3.14 is very new; hosting providers and packages may lag. Also consider generating requirements with the same major/minor Python version you deploy to, because `pip freeze` from 3.14 can include versions or environment markers that are awkward on 3.11. Ideally your local deploy venv should match production.Yes — if DreamHost only offers Python 3.10, the cleanest path is to make your **production-compatible local venv use Python 3.10** and generate `requirements.txt` from that, not from your Python 3.14 venv.
> 
> For `numpy`, the issue is probably that your local `pip freeze` captured a version requiring newer Python. Use a version compatible with 3.10. For example:
> 
> ```bash
> cd "$HOME/projects/arachne"
> 
> # Create a production-compatible local venv.
> python3.10 -m venv venv310
> venv310/bin/python -m pip install --upgrade pip setuptools wheel
> 
> # Install deps, pinning numpy if needed.
> venv310/bin/python -m pip install "numpy<2.3"
> venv310/bin/python -m pip install -r requirements.in  # if you have one
> ```
> 
> If you only have `requirements.txt`, edit or constrain the problematic line:
> 
> ```text
> numpy<2.3
> ```
> 
> or more conservatively:
> 
> ```text
> numpy==2.2.6
> ```
> 
> `numpy 2.2.x` supports Python 3.10; newer NumPy lines may not.
> 
> Then regenerate your deployment requirements from the Python 3.10 venv:
> 
> ```bash
> venv310/bin/python -m pip freeze > requirements.txt
> git add requirements.txt
> git commit -m "Pin dependencies for Python 3.10 production"
> git push origin master
> ```
> 
> I would also change your deployment script config from:
> 
> ```bash
> LOCAL_VENV_PATH="$LOCAL_PATH/venv2026"
> ```
> 
> to:
> 
> ```bash
> LOCAL_VENV_PATH="$LOCAL_PATH/venv310"
> REMOTE_PYTHON="python3.10"
> ```
> 
> and create the remote venv explicitly with:
> 
> ```bash
> python3.10 -m venv venv
> ```
> 
> rather than `python3 -m venv venv`.
> 
> My stronger recommendation: stop using raw `pip freeze` from your daily development environment as the production dependency source. Use either:
> 
> - `requirements.in` for human-chosen top-level packages, plus `pip-compile` to produce `requirements.txt`, or
> - a dedicated `venv310` solely for production compatibility.
> 
> That way your Python 3.14 experiments won’t accidentally break DreamHost deployment.
